keywords:
  - analytic number theory
  - Riemann zeta function
  - explicit formula
  - Weil positivity
  - conductor bounds
  - Fourier interpolation
  - computer-assisted proof
abstract: >-
  We study the positive solutions of Weil's explicit formula with the archimedean data of the Riemann zeta
  function: pairs of positive measures, one on the zero side and one on the prime side beyond log 2/(2π). By
  linear-programming duality such pairs exist if and only if a positivity statement involving neither zeros nor
  primes holds; its slack κ* gives the optimal degree-one explicit-formula conductor bound q_min = exp(−2πκ*). We
  conjecture that ζ's own pair is the only solution; this does not imply the Riemann hypothesis, but makes it
  equivalent to existence. We prove the conjecture for pairs carried by the zeros of ζ plus finitely many points
  and by the Bondarenko–Radchenko–Seip nodes, using positivity of the prime measure only on one residue class of
  nodes, and of the zero measure only at the origin. Certificates of the form Ξ²(H + ε·exp(−πt²)), with H a
  polynomial in t², vanish at every zero of ζ and show unconditionally that κ* ≤ 1.1508391·10⁻⁹⁰⁶. Uniqueness
  would follow from asymptotic properties of a family of such functions, defined by Hermite interpolation at the
  prime powers.
